  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Frequency Components",
    "lesson_core_move": "Decompose a real sine or cosine into paired complex exponentials at positive and negative angular frequencies and interpret their conjugate coefficients as counter-rotating phasor components.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Frequency Components",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Positive and Negative Frequency Components",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the phasor view, $e^{j\\omega t}$ is the positive-frequency component and $e^{-j\\omega t}$ is the negative-frequency component. For a real sinusoid, read the signal as a matched $+\\omega$/$-\\omega$ pair whose imaginary parts cancel.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\omega t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(e^{j\\omega t}+e^{-j\\omega t}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cleanest base case is $\\cos(\\omega t)$. Its split is a sum of equal coefficients at $+\\omega$ and $-\\omega$, so the two complex exponentials form a conjugate pair and the result stays real.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sin(\\omega t)=\\frac{1}{2j}\\left(e^{j\\omega t}-e^{-j\\omega t}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "$\\sin(\\omega t)$ uses the same two-frequency split. The factor $1/j$ only changes the coefficients, not the frequency labels, so the $+\\omega$ and $-\\omega$ terms still appear as a conjugate pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)=\\frac{A}{2}e^{j\\phi}e^{j\\omega t}+\\frac{A}{2}e^{-j\\phi}e^{-j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When amplitude and phase are added, keep the time factors $e^{j\\omega t}$ and $e^{-j\\omega t}$ fixed and move the phase into the coefficients. That gives a positive-frequency component with coefficient $\\frac{A}{2}e^{j\\phi}$ and a negative-frequency component with coefficient $\\frac{A}{2}e^{-j\\phi}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)=\\frac{A}{2j}e^{j\\phi}e^{j\\omega t}-\\frac{A}{2j}e^{-j\\phi}e^{-j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same rule works for $A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)$. The coefficients still come in matched $+\\omega$ and $-\\omega$ terms, and the pair remains conjugate even though the common scale includes $1/j$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The reusable rule is simple: a real sinusoid must have matched $+\\omega$ and $-\\omega$ components with conjugate coefficients, and those two terms are the counter-rotating phasor components of the signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Frequency Components",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Decomposing a Cosine into Positive and Negative Frequency Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $3\\cos(4t-\\pi/6)$ as a sum of positive- and negative-frequency complex exponentials.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a cosine with both amplitude and phase, so start from the cosine conjugate-pair identity from the previous section. Keep the $+\\omega$ and $-\\omega$ terms separate and move the phase into the coefficients.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)}+e^{-j(\\omega t+\\phi)}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $\\omega=4$, $A=3$, and $\\phi=-\\pi/6$. Substituting those values gives one positive-frequency term at $+4$ and one negative-frequency term at $-4$, with the phase carried by the coefficients.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3\\cos(4t-\\pi/6)=\\frac{3}{2}e^{-j\\pi/6}e^{j4t}+\\frac{3}{2}e^{j\\pi/6}e^{-j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The $+4$ component has coefficient $\\frac{3}{2}e^{-j\\pi/6}$, and the $-4$ component has the conjugate coefficient $\\frac{3}{2}e^{j\\pi/6}$. Because the coefficients are conjugates, the two counter-rotating components sum to the real cosine.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Frequency Components",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Decomposing a Sine into Positive and Negative Frequency Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $4\\sin(2t+\\pi/4)$ as a sum of positive- and negative-frequency complex exponentials.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a sine with amplitude and phase, so switch from the cosine identity to the sine identity and keep the $+\\omega$ and $-\\omega$ terms explicit. The only new wrinkle relative to the cosine example is the $1/j$ factor and the subtraction between the two frequency components.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)=\\frac{1}{2j}\\left(e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)}-e^{-j(\\omega t+\\phi)}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $\\omega=2$, $A=4$, and $\\phi=\\pi/4$. Substitute those values, then split each exponential into a coefficient and a frequency-sign term without changing the notation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4\\sin(2t+\\pi/4)=\\frac{4}{2j}e^{j\\pi/4}e^{j2t}-\\frac{4}{2j}e^{-j\\pi/4}e^{-j2t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The $+2$ component has coefficient $\\frac{4}{2j}e^{j\\pi/4}$, and the $-2$ component has the conjugate coefficient $-\\frac{4}{2j}e^{-j\\pi/4}$. The subtraction is exactly what makes the two counter-rotating components combine to the real sine.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Frequency Components",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Identifying Conjugate Coefficients in a Real Sinusoid",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(1-j)e^{j3t}+\\frac{5}{2}(1+j)e^{-j3t}$, identify the positive-frequency coefficient, the negative-frequency coefficient, and whether the signal is real.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a pair of complex exponentials at $+3$ and $-3$, so read the coefficients directly from the terms rather than expanding anything. This is exactly the conjugate-pair test from the previous sections.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(1-j)e^{j3t}+\\frac{5}{2}(1+j)e^{-j3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The coefficient of $e^{j3t}$ is the positive-frequency coefficient, so the $+3$ component is $\\frac{5}{2}(1-j)$. The coefficient of $e^{-j3t}$ is the negative-frequency coefficient, so the $-3$ component is $\\frac{5}{2}(1+j)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{5}{2}(1+j)=\\left(\\frac{5}{2}(1-j)\\right)^{*}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two coefficients are complex conjugates, so the pair is conjugate-symmetric. That means this expression can represent a real sinusoid, with the two terms acting as counter-rotating phasor components.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Frequency Components",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Counter-Rotating Phasor Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Interpret the components of $x(t)=\\frac{A}{2}e^{j\\phi}e^{j\\omega t}+\\frac{A}{2}e^{-j\\phi}e^{-j\\omega t}$ as two phasors rotating in opposite directions.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the sign in the exponential. The $e^{j\\omega t}$ term is the positive-frequency phasor, so it rotates counterclockwise at angular speed $\\omega$. The $e^{-j\\omega t}$ term is the negative-frequency phasor, so it rotates clockwise at the same speed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{A}{2}e^{j\\phi}e^{j\\omega t}+\\frac{A}{2}e^{-j\\phi}e^{-j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The coefficients are conjugates, $\\frac{A}{2}e^{j\\phi}$ and $\\frac{A}{2}e^{-j\\phi}$. That symmetry means the two rotating vectors are mirror images about the real axis, which is the signature of a real-valued sinusoid.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left(\\frac{A}{2}e^{j\\phi}\\right)^{*}=\\frac{A}{2}e^{-j\\phi}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you add the two components, their imaginary parts cancel at every time. What remains is a real signal built from the real-axis projections of the counter-rotating phasors.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
